  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1143 AM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nEarlier this morning an upper level disturbance embedded in the\nsouthwesterly flow produced a few high based sprinkles/showers \nas it crossed western Kansas. Two more upper level waves were \npresent in this southwest flow over the Southwest United States.\nModels are in generally in good agreement with the next upper \nwave crossing southwest Kansas by early Tuesday. This will \nquickly be followed by the next upper wave Tuesday night. \nMoisture return early this week will be limited but given the \nelevated precipitation that did develop earlier this morning \nwould not be surprised to see another round of sprinkles or even\na few widely scatted thunderstorms with either of these upper \nwaves, especially from the stronger upper wave Tuesday night.\nFollowing these upper level disturbances an upper ridge will \nthen build across the Central Plains as warmer temperatures \nbegin to spread across western Kansas. This will mark the \nbeginning to the warmup that will occur across southwest Kansas.\n\nUnseasonably warm temperatures across southwest Kansas through \nthe end of the work week. An upper level low off the west coast \nwill deepen as it drops south, west of the California coast, mid\nweek which will result in an upper ridge building into the \nPlains. Improving westerly downslope flow will result in \nunseasonably warm 850mb temperatures (near 20C) returning to \nsouthwest Kansas Wednesday and Thursday. Given good run to run \nagreement with these 850mb temperatures spreading back into \nsouthwest Kansas and a small 25th to 75th percentile spread in \nthe the NBM forecast max temperatures it appears that the \ncurrent forecast still looks on track with highs in the upper \n70s to lower 80s for much of southwest Kansas with the warmest\ntemperatures this week being Wednesday and Thursday.\n\nGusty winds will be possible this week with wind gusts in \nexcess of 40 mph possible on Friday. A trough of low pressure at\nthe surface will deepen over eastern Colorado which will result\nin a strengthening pressure gradient developing across \nsouthwest Kansas late week. This combined with stronger winds \naloft (mean ensemble cluster 850mb wind speeds Friday ranging \nfrom 40 to near 45 knots) should easily support the NBM guidance\non wind gust ranging from 35 to 45 mph. Also there is a chance\n(20-30% chance) that some locations west of highway 83 could be\nas high as 45 to near 50 mph based deep mixing potential and \n850-700mb wind speeds Friday afternoon.\n\nCooler, more seasonal, temperatures return to southwest Kansas \nover the weekend as precipitation chances improve Friday night. \nLate this week the upper low off the coast of California mid \nweek will move east across the southwest United States and \nbegin to weaken. As it does the upper ridge axis over the plains\nwill also move east into the Mississippi Valley. The developing\nsouthwesterly flow aloft ahead of this approaching upper trough\nwill allow moisture to return to southwest Kansas late week \nbased on improving PWATS. Based on this combined with a cold \nfront moving south across the Central Plains will result in \nimproving chances for thunderstorms. Currently it appears the \nbest chance for thunderstorms across southwest Kansas will be \nFriday night through Sunday. This will be followed by cooler, \nmore seasonal temperatures.",
